\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Finite changes of references and the product subgroup}

\p{Let #{G_p} be groups indexed by rational primes, with subgroups
#{U_p,U'_p\leq G_p} equal outside a finite set #{F}. Write
#{G_f(U)} and #{G_f(U')} for their
\vocabk{restricted products}{fcap-004F}, regarded here as groups
of tuples, and #{K(U)=\prod_pU_p}, #{K(U')=\prod_pU'_p} for
their \vocabk{product reference subgroups}{fcap-004F}.
The coordinate identity gives a group isomorphism
#{e:G_f(U)\to G_f(U')}, but

##{e(K(U))=K(U')\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
U_p=U'_p\ \text{for every prime }p.}}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{If #{x_p\in U_p} outside a finite set #{S}, then
#{x_p\in U'_p} outside #{S\cup F}. Interchanging the two
families proves the converse. Thus the two groups have the same
tuples and coordinate operations, giving the stated isomorphism.}

\p{Everywhere equality of references immediately gives equality
of their products. For the converse, suppose #{e(K(U))=K(U')}.
Fix a prime #{p} and #{u\in U_p}. The tuple with component #{u}
at #{p} and identity at every other prime belongs to #{K(U)}.
Its image is the same tuple in #{K(U')}, so #{u\in U'_p}.
This proves #{U_p\subseteq U'_p}. Interchange the products and
repeat the argument to obtain #{U'_p\subseteq U_p}. Since #{p}
was arbitrary, all reference subgroups agree.}
}

\p{In particular, finite changes need not preserve even the number
of double cosets. Take #{G_p=C_2=\{1,s\}} at every prime, with
#{s^2=1\ne s}, and take #{U_p=C_2} for every #{p}. Change only
#{U'_2=\{1\}}, keeping #{U'_p=C_2} for #{p\ne2}. Both restricted
products are #{\prod_pC_2}: the changed condition can fail only at
#{2}. With left subgroup #{\Gamma=\{1\}}, the quotient by
#{K(U)=\prod_pC_2} has one class. The quotient by #{K(U')} has
exactly two classes, distinguished by the #{2}-coordinate.
Indeed, right multiplication by #{K(U')} preserves that coordinate;
if #{x_2=y_2}, then #{x^{-1}y\in K(U')} and #{y=x(x^{-1}y)}.
Both coordinates #{1,s} occur. This is a group-family example,
without an asserted realization by adelic O, SO or Spin groups.}

\p{For two compatible tuples #{U,U'} for the same rational
quadratic form, fix #{H\in\{O,SO,\Spin\}} and suppose their
#{H}-references agree outside a finite set. The coordinate identity
#{e_H} identifies the ambient groups #{H_f(U)} and #{H_f(U')}
and carries their actual rational diagonal images onto each other:
both scalar extensions of any rational #{h} have the same
coordinate #{h_p} at every prime. Thus \ref{fcap-004S} gives

##{\Gamma_H(U)\backslash H_f(U)/K_H(U)
\simeq
\Gamma_H(U')\backslash H_f(U')/e_H(K_H(U)).}

The right subgroup displayed in the target is the transported
product, which equals #{K_H(U')} exactly when all the
#{H}-references agree. Eventual equality alone does not supply
the hypotheses for transport to #{\mathcal C_H(U')} with its
chosen right subgroup. This does not assert that different right
subgroups always give nonisomorphic class sets. The O, derived SO
and generated Spin cases each retain their own subgroups.}

\p{Finite-stage adelic constructions provide the background in
\citet{Theorem 3.6, pp. 6-8}{conrad2011adelic}; the product-subgroup
criterion and counterexample above are direct deductions.
TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.map_integralSubgroup_restrictedProductCongr_eq_iff}
formalizes the criterion, and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.doubleCosetCongr}
transports double cosets to the image subgroups. Neither
statement replaces that image with a different product subgroup.}
